# Simple first, compatible always

Default to the **simplest solution that works everywhere**. Only reach for newer or cleverer techniques when the desired effect is **literally impossible** (or meaningfully worse) with plain HTML/CSS/JS.

## Decision order

1. **Plain markup** — semantic HTML, inline SVG, `<img>`, normal elements
2. **Basic CSS** — colors, borders, padding, `currentColor`, flex/grid
3. **Small includes/partials** — when the same markup repeats (Jekyll `_includes/`)
4. **Modern APIs last** — masks, `color-mix`, `light-dark()`, view transitions, etc. — only if simpler options cannot do the job

## When fancy is OK

Use advanced CSS/JS only when you can state why simpler options fail, e.g.:

- OS-driven theming without duplicate assets → `light-dark()` / `color-scheme`
- A decorative pattern that cannot be a plain border or inline SVG → masked/repeating background
- Client-side behavior that HTML alone cannot provide → small, focused script

If a simple approach gets 95% of the way there, prefer it unless the user explicitly wants the extra polish.

## Avoid

- CSS masks/filters when inline SVG + `currentColor` or a styled `<img>` suffices
- Pseudo-elements duplicating what a child element would do clearly
- Custom properties + indirection when a direct style works
- Abstractions for one-off use (helpers, wrappers, config) without reuse
- Separate asset files **and** inline copies of the same icon/SVG

## Example from this project

```html
<!-- ❌ BAD — mask + CSS variable + empty span + external SVG -->
<span class="social-icon" style="--icon: url('/assets/icons/github.svg')"></span>

<!-- ✅ GOOD — inline SVG, themed with currentColor -->
<span class="social-icon" aria-hidden="true">
  <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path fill="currentColor" d="…"/></svg>
</span>
```

```css
/* ✅ GOOD — square + icon, no masks */
.social-icon {
  background: var(--text);
  color: var(--bg);
  padding: 0.18em;
}
.social-icon svg { width: 100%; height: 100%; }
```

## Before adding complexity, ask

- Can this be done with normal elements and a few lines of CSS?
- Will this work in older browsers without polyfills or fallbacks?
- Am I adding moving parts (files, vars, JS) that a human would struggle to edit later?

If yes to the first two and no to the third, keep it simple.
